A||||3||Jncomutt||E-Brake Doesn't Hold Car!||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||04-28-2001||11:23 PM||||Just changed my rear pads and now my ebrake doesn't work.  I tried adjusting the equalizer and its tightened to the max.  Even still, when I pull up the lever, car rolls freely.  Everything is connected and hooked up.  Is there something else I should check or need to adjust??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-28-2001||11:30 PM||||The hand brake can take awhile to settle in after a pad change. Read some of the other recent hand brake thread in this section for aids that may help.<P>If the brake doesn't settle in....<P>take the wheels off and see if the levers are returning to the stops.<P>You could need to rebuild the calipers and/or replace the cables.<P>Also, If the lever nut on the caliper has been over torqued then the lever may have been damaged and may be binding. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-29-2001||04:17 AM||||To set the rear pads after you do a brake job, you are suppose to hold the brake pedal down and "pump" the E-brake a few times to set things. If it still doesn't hold then something is wrong. Follow Ogre's list to correct.<BR>BTW ~ What pads did you use? Some of the carbon metallic pads tend to be "slick" when cold so the E-brake doesn't work as well.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||04-29-2001||12:17 PM||||I have found that you must compress the piston back into the caliper (with piston tool if you have one) and hold it there while you install the E-brake lever. If you already have the caliper on the car, then take a screwdriver (with e-brake lever removed) and put in the caliper and compress the piston back in and hold it there while you install the e-brake lever.||||reg||1
